SM(200,{"n":"Philippians 1","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/Philippians 1.mp3","d":3134.2,"s":[[0.0,"Chapter 1, verse 1."],[7.28,"Paul and Timothy, bondservants of Jesus Christ, to all the saints in Christ Jesus who are in Philippi with the bishops and deacons."],[20.92,"So we already mentioned that this is coming from Saint Paul and Saint Timothy."],[26.28,"He has a co-author."],[29.24,"Bondservants is a fancy way of saying slaves."],[33.56,"That's really how Saints Paul and Timothy are describing themselves as being slaves of Christ."],[44.2,"They're writing to all the saints."],[46.04,"Remember, that's to all the holy ones."],[48.2,"We've talked before about how that language of holy ones is what's applied in the Old Testament to the angels."],[55.88,"the To angelic beings."],[59.41,"We've seen how in I think every single one of St."],[63.97,"Paul's epistles that we've read so far, he begins with those identifications where when he's talking to the people, he reminds them of who they are in Christ, and therefore who they're called to be in Christ."],[74.77,"So he uses this language of them being sons of God, which is language applied to the angels in the Old Testament."],[80.61,"He uses this language of holy ones, which is applied to the angels in the Old Testament, all of which is to is to say, This is who you are in Christ."],[91.33,"This is what you're called to be."],[92.69,"So we saw in 1st and 2nd Corinthians he addressed them that way."],[95.93,"Even though that wasn't who they were acting like."],[99.51,"That was part of their call to be."],[100.15,"Paul does the same here"],[102.51,"So St. The difference here is he particularly addresses the leadership."],[115.09,"The leaders of the community, the bishops and the deacons."],[117.73,"And the word translated bishop here is episcopos."],[121.41,"Usually translate as bishop."],[123.81,"That literally means sometimes it's translated in English overseer, because that's doing a very literalistic translation."],[132.29,"That scopos in Episcopos is the same word we get scope from."],[140.59,"Not the mouthwash, but scope like you look through."],[143.41,"Telescope. A P means over or upon"],[146.25,"And so that literally means to see."],[151.69,"So the word in Greek was used in the Greek Old Testament to translate Raim, which is a similar word that comes from a verb of seeing, to watch over, that was used for herdsmen, like shepherds and herdsmen."],[171.45,"So that's the idea in episcopos."],[174.09,"It's not overseer, like we would think of an overseer."],[177.58,"Like an overseer of slaves or or a a boss on a job."],[182.11,"It's uh more like a shepherd or a herdsman."],[186.19,"That's why it gets translated as past.or, shepherd into Latin"],[191.27,"You're sounding a different word used for something like that, like slave master or boss."],[196.87,"Well, yeah, you'd get a word like master, like like despota."],[201.05,"Which we also call bishops, but that's more the idea of master or lord, curios."],[206.22,"A lord or master."],[207.9,"Um so this is this is the shepherds, and that that idea has a rich history of the Old Testament."],[216.08,"Where the leaders of Israel are the shepherds of Israel."],[219.25,"They're not the ones who own the sheep."],[221.65,"They're God's sheep, but he has these shepherds who he's sent to watch over the sheep."],[227.67,"And so that same idea is included in the idea of bishops."],[231.23,"Paul, that's how he's addressing them"],[232.23,"So St. You're the shepherds, you're the herdsmen who are over Christ's flock."],[241.83,"Who are doing this job."],[243.59,"Deacon, of course, comes from Diaconia service, diaconos, a servant."],[251.64,"And so there are people who are serving in the community, and there are people who've been assigned to oversee and watch over, guide, protect the community."],[266.68,"That's interesting. Because I mean, we can't, we can never say for sure why St."],[272.04,"Paul didn't choose to include, yeah, the. presbyters, because there were presbyters"],[277.76,"But the the role of presbyter at this point, um, was that they were uh essentially every community had an overseer."],[294.14,"An episcopos who was essentially functioning as the head presbyter, and then a plurality of presbyters around that person, and how much that person who was the episcopus, the bishop, was differentiated from the rest of the presbyters, was different in different Christian communities."],[316.54,"So in Rome until about the third. century, he was basically the head presbyter"],[324.1,"Sorry, Roman Catholics, I'll pick on them this time."],[327.22,"There was not a strong episcopate in Rome in the first and second centuries."],[334.34,"The same thing was true in Alexandria for even longer."],[339.06,"All the way into the fourth century in Alexandria, the actual bishop of Alexandria had less power than the presbyters meeting in council."],[352.62,"Then there were other places like Antioch, where already at the end of the first century was Saint Ignatius, the episcopal office is clearly a unique, different office and had unique authority, and the presbyters were basically seen as exercising his authority in his absence, the way it is today in the Orthodox Church."],[373.74,"That was already true the first century in Antioch."],[376.86,"But it it differed in different cities in the early in the early centuries."],[382.14,"I love using St."],[383.34,"Ignatius of Antioch in some of the his quotes."],[385.98,"I showed them to Protestants and I asked them, can you guess which century this is from?"],[390.54,"Yeah. Is this first century?"],[392.22,"They go. Yeah."],[394.14,"What? Yeah. Yeah."],[396.22,"As as one when I was in Protestant seminary, as my church history professor put it, uh, by the end of the first century, in St."],[407.5,"Ignatius of Antioch. you have all of the features of the magisterial bishop"],[413.94,"How did things go so wrong so fast?"],[418.18,"And I said, you know, there's another way of interpreting that."],[422.98,"Even then, as a Protestant, I said, you know, there's another way of interpreting that."],[427.32,"That maybe they did go wrong."],[429.17,"Um, but so that that would what I'm getting to is that may very well have been the case in um in Philippi, where the the there may not have been a strong separate role of presbyter at this point."],[450.61,"And that may be why."],[451.57,"Like I said, we can never know for sure why Saint Paul chose yeah yeah he may be using that to just as just a general term for leadership also is also possible so he may be including the presbyters and the actual bishop may have been sort of a head presbyter it's hard to know for sure but there were a variety of and and the part of the reason for that especially in the first century is that the apostles were still around."],[484.79,"So when when Saint Paul uh visits Ephesus on his way to Jerusalem at the end of the book of Acts, he's he meets with the presbyters at Ephesus."],[495.39,"at Well, why wouldn't there have been a bishop Ephesus?"],[495.63,"Ephesus Well, there were multiple apostles coming in and out of, including Saint Paul."],[504.62,"And so Saint John, was around Ephesus at least."],[509.62,"St. Mary Magdalene, the Theotokos was in Ephesus for a while."],[514.18,"And so when you had apostolic leadership, you'd always have presbyters."],[523.04,"But uh you wouldn't necessarily have."],[525.52,"And so the reason sometimes you'll see, like in history texts, they'll refer to the bishops as the successors to the apostles, is that it's really once the apostles are gone that the role of the episcopacy sort of consolidates."],[542.22,"And to and takes off, because uh you don't have the sort of direct leader of the leadership of the apostles anymore."],[550.78,"And we'll see when we get into first and second Timothy."],[554.91,"That when St."],[555.95,"Paul sends Saint Timothy to Crete, he's gonna say, and all of the churches and all of the communities appoint presbyters and deacons."],[565.07,"Well, what's Saint Timothy then?"],[569.79,"I mean, he's really an apostle, we consider him one of the 70."],[573.01,"But he's also sort of fulfilling that episcopal office."],[576.63,"He's clearly not a presbyter because he's going around appointing presbyters, he's clearly something else."],[582.64,"Um, and so we we see that starting to happen with St."],[587.12,"Paul and St."],[587.76,"Timothy, where St."],[588.64,"Timothy is sort of this pivotal figure because he's he's an apostle in one sense, but he's also sort of a bishop in a sense."],[594.48,"And then his successors are are just going to be bishops."],[598.69,"Um so yeah."],[600.61,"So all that's to say we don't know exactly, but it's likely related to the fact that the precise offices were kind of malleable at this point, in terms of how they were in any particular place."],[615.41,"Um but uh also this is this is the only one of St."],[619.01,"Paul's epistles where he directly mentions the leadership in the opening."],[624.29,"I mean, there are places where he mentions leaders later. on by name, but this is one"],[630.73,"So as we're going to see, this is another, because I said this is going to be a more positive letter."],[639.37,"He's going to focus a little more on teaching, and so it makes sense that he would direct his teaching to the leaders who are going to be teaching the people."],[651.35,"So that kind of makes sense that this would be the letter where he does that."],[658.07,"Verse 2, grace to you and peace from God our Father and the Lord Jesus Christ."],[661.83,"We've seen that's pretty standard in terms of St."],[665.51,"Paul's opening greeting."],[669.59,"Verse 3, I thank my God upon every remembrance of you, always in every prayer of mine, making request for for you all with joy."],[679.55,"For your fellowship in the gospel from the first day until now."],[685.07,"So you can already see."],[687.17,"Remember I said there's a different tone?"],[689.86,"So this isn't like Galatians, where it's like how readily you've received another gospel."],[695.06,"Uh this isn't like 1 Corinthians, where in chapter 1 he sort of launched into the, hey, this guy's uh engaged in unrepentant sexual immorality."],[706.18,"What's up with that?"],[707.31,"Um it's not even Romans where he starts goes into, you know, Romans 1, he started talking about the wrath of God like being revealed on immorality."],[713.95,"Here we have how much St"],[720.47,"Paul is giving thanks for them with joy."],[724.04,"Uh every and he says, he's saying, every time I pray, and then every prayer I say."],[729.74,"He mentions the Philippians and gives thanks for them and joyfully requests good things for them."],[737.74,"And uh the word fellowship there is is uh Kenania communion."],[743.56,"In the gospel of Jesus Christ."],[745.24,"For the first day, from the day he met them when he first went there, all the way until now, he's felt this close communion and fellowship with them."],[755.0,"Uh that's significant because. again, some of the communities he wrote to, he hasn't been able to say this"],[763.66,"With Rome, he didn't know he hadn't met them yet."],[766.39,"So he didn't have this communion with them."],[768.63,"Uh the Corinthians, he knew very well, but that communion had kind of been broken by the things that were going on."],[775.83,"But St. Paul's saying how much he rejoices in that."],[779.21,"How good that is that they've had this kind of unbroken communion from the very beginning."],[788.33,"Being confident of this very thing that he who has begun a good work in you will complete it until the day of Jesus Christ."],[797.71,"So these um there's a little bit more in that phrase than you might immediately notice because he said the day of Jesus Christ."],[806.91,"Now we all know instinctively what he's talking about."],[810.16,"He's talking about the last day, the day Christ returns, the day of the Lord."],[816.4,"No, that is what he means."],[817.84,"That is what he means."],[818.88,"But here's why that's significant."],[821.92,"Because, see, we're so used to seeing the day of the Lord, and he refers to the Lord Jesus Christ all the time, that it doesn't strike us."],[830.56,"But if we were reading the Old Testament in Hebrew, it wouldn't say day of the Lord."],[838.08,"It says the day of Yahweh."],[840.48,"That's what the last day was called."],[843.12,"That's what the day of judgment was called."],[844.64,"It was called the day of Yahweh, the God of Israel."],[849.4,"So that gets transliterated as day of the Lord, because you don't find, like in Greek and stuff, the uh name of name Yahweh being transliterated directly."],[864.3,"So it gets translated, they put Lord, Kyrgios, as a as a replacement."],[868.59,"So when St."],[869.39,"Paul calls it the day of the Lord, in the New Testament, that's ambiguous."],[874.69,"Does he mean the day of the Lord like the Lord Jesus, or does he mean the day of the Lord like the day of Yahweh?"],[881.67,"But here he calls the day of Yahweh the day of Jesus Christ."],[888.41,"Which means St."],[889.69,"Paul is using those interchangeably, which tells us something about who Saint Paul thinks Jesus is."],[899.9,"We're Orthodox Christians, so the idea that Jesus Christ is God is not a big deal."],[904.54,"Okay. But if you were a skeptical modern theologian, you would reject the idea that St."],[910.78,"Paul thought that."],[912.82,"But here's one more piece of evidence."],[914.98,"We've seen a bunch as we've been going through St."],[917.3,"Paul's epistles. Here's one more piece of evidence that he's not just thinking that Jesus Christ is the Son of God or in some way connected to Yahweh, the God of Israel."],[929.86,"He's identifying. Jesus with Yahweh the God of Israel"],[934.86,"In some sense, where he thinks he can say that interchangeably."],[941.5,"And notice how he describes their fellowship in the gospel."],[949.02,"He doesn't describe their fellowship in the gospel as you and me are both born again."],[954.62,"You both got saved."],[955.66,"I got saved on that road to Damascus."],[957.42,"You guys got saved when I showed up there and preached the gospel to you."],[960.7,"And you made a decision."],[963.36,"Is that what he says?"],[964.96,"He says, our fellowship in the gospel is that God has begun a good work in you."],[971.84,"God has begun your salvation in you."],[974.4,"you Once get baptized?"],[974.88,"No And he's going to bring that to completion when?"],[982.28,"Once you do enough good works?"],[983.88,"No. Once you're in the state of grace?"],[986.26,"No. That's going to come to its conclusion on the day of the Lord."],[992.02,"Until then, we're working on it."],[996.64,"Until then, we're working on it."],[998.08,"He's begun it."],[999.36,"He's gonna bring it to completion."],[1001.92,"We're gonna see in uh chapter three."],[1005.12,"He's gonna. We've talked before several times about how St."],[1008.24,"Paul sort of drops the themes he's gonna talk about later into the beginning of the letter."],[1013.52,"This is one of the themes he's gonna expand on later in the letter."],[1017.28,"What he means by this."],[1018.72,"But it's important to note here this theme that for St."],[1021.72,"Paul, fellowship and communion in the gospel, salvation, is not this thing that happened once."],[1036.28,"But then carries not only through our life, but until we enter into the next life at the resurrection."],[1055.4,"Verse 7 Just as it is right for me to think this of you all, because I have you in my heart, inasmuch as both in my chains and in the defense and confirmation of the gospel, you all are partakers with me of grace."],[1075.68,"So St. Paul is saying, I've got you here with me in my heart, whether I'm here in chains, or when I'm out there preaching, preaching the gospel, traveling from place to place."],[1094.78,"Are receiving the same grace."],[1096.7,"And we've talked about what grace is."],[1098.06,"Grace is God acting."],[1100.15,"Meaning, just as Christ has begun this work in you and is working in your life, he's working in my life."],[1106.55,"Whether I'm sitting here in a prison or whether I'm out there preaching the gospel."],[1110.71,"And we have this in common."],[1112.31,"This is what that communion in the gospel is that God is working in all of us and in each of us, to bring this work of our salvation to completion."],[1122.01,"We're all on this road together, headed in the same direction."],[1127.61,"And that gives St."],[1128.97,"Paul this unity with them, regardless of his circumstances, and causes him to rejoice regardless of his circumstances."],[1143.73,"For God is my witness, how greatly I long for you all with the affection of Jesus Christ."],[1150.47,"So he's saying, I wish I could be with you in person."],[1155.05,"That's not just because he's now in prison and doesn't want to be in prison, but be actually them."],[1159.77,"Because remember, these are people he does know."],[1163.82,"Verse 9, and this I pray that your love may abound still more and more in knowledge and in all discernment."],[1174.7,"So he's saying he's already said he makes these requests for them with joy."],[1180.71,"Now he's saying, This is what I'm asking for for you."],[1183.99,"This is what I want for you."],[1189.35,"That their love would abound more and more."],[1192.25,"Their love for each other, their love for God."],[1194.57,"But what form does that love take for St"],[1198.66,"Paul? It takes the form of knowledge and discernment."],[1205.62,"Okay, we'll talk about what those two things mean."],[1208.58,"But this is important in terms of the kind of lazy way we use the term love in the modern world."],[1220.18,"So when he says, I want you guys to be more and more loving."],[1226.47,"We tend to associate being loving or being kind to people with being ignorant and being undiscerning."],[1236.79,"What do I mean?"],[1239.05,"What do I mean?"],[1240.73,"Well, we want more love in our community."],[1243.05,"What does that mean?"],[1244.09,"That means, well, we just welcome anybody."],[1248.41,"No matter who they are, no matter what they're doing, no matter how they act, no matter how they're living their life."],[1256.81,"We just welcome them and love them anyway."],[1261.41,"And to help us do that, in terms of the ignorance, we don't ask a lot of questions."],[1267.71,"We just don't want to know about that stuff."],[1270.43,"Because that might make it harder to love you."],[1273.81,"So we're all just, we're not going to talk about any stuff that might upset anybody."],[1279.09,"And we're not gonna ever confront anybody about anything."],[1281.01,"That's the opposite of what St"],[1283.13,"And now, see, we're super loving."],[1287.03,"Paul's talking about."],[1288.65,"That's why he qualifies it."],[1291.3,"So, what does love have to do with knowledge?"],[1294.66,"Well, you can't love God more and more without coming to know him more and more."],[1299.72,"That's what causes you to love him more and more."],[1302.52,"Not know about him."],[1304.36,"This isn't knowledge, again, of brain, you know, learning facts."],[1309.74,"And I've used this as an example a bunch of times before."],[1312.38,"There are probably people in the world who know more facts about my wife than I do."],[1317.92,"Her doctor probably knows more facts about her than I do."],[1322.84,"Because her doctor, she knows like all her blood levels of everything, and you know, exactly how much she weighs and exactly how tall she is to the fraction of an inch and all the data that's in her chart."],[1334.78,"That I don't know, but her doctor knows."],[1337.19,"Knows more about her."],[1338.57,"There are people who have known her longer than I have known her, who know about stuff she did in her childhood and said and things that I don't know about."],[1348.06,"But I'd be willing to bet money that there's not someone who knows my wife better than I do."],[1355.36,"Because there's a difference between knowing someone as a person and knowing a bunch of facts about someone."],[1363.36,"And so this isn't talking about coming to know more facts about God."],[1367.96,"This doesn't mean like, wow, I hope you learn about the attributes of God and how God is pure act and how his divine simplicity relates to the essence and energies distinction."],[1381.32,"And that's not what he's talking about."],[1383.82,"He's not talking about what we call theology."],[1386.3,"He's talking about coming to know God, coming to know Christ."],[1390.62,"As we all as Orthodox Christians sort of learn from St."],[1393.1,"Gregory Palamas, that doesn't require you to go to school even a single day."],[1398.87,"To come close to God and come to know him."],[1401.75,"So that's the kind of knowledge he's talking about, but not just toward God, but toward each other."],[1408.49,"Toward each other."],[1410.21,"So our love of each other is not based on ignoring things about each other."],[1415.39,"Or deliberately not asking questions that we think we might not like the answer to, but it's learning to love each other, getting to know each other better, and love each other."],[1426.53,"That's what he wants for them."],[1429.01,"And then discernment, of course, is the opposite of just accepting whatever."],[1437.03,"He's about to expand on that."],[1439.19,"So I'll read the next verse."],[1441.11,"That you may approve the things that are excellent, that you may be sincere and without offense till the day of Christ."],[1449.11,"Being filled with the fruits of righteousness, which are by Jesus Christ. to the glory and praise of God"],[1455.76,"So discernment, see, this positive tone."],[1460.74,"Discernment isn't just about identifying what is bad."],[1465.96,"That's how we tend to look at it."],[1467.72,"So you'll get people who this will be the juxtaposition they give you."],[1471.72,"Either you're loving and you just accept whatever."],[1475.96,"Or you're judgmental."],[1478.68,"And you're always pointing out faults and pointing out bad."],[1483.46,"You're shady. This is a false dilemma."],[1487.08,"Because St. Paul is pointing out what discernment actually is."],[1491.8,"Discernment is knowing what is good."],[1496.21,"Knowing what is good, knowing what is excellent, knowing what is pure, knowing what is holy, identifying those things and seeking after those things."],[1506.21,"And that's not just in your life and your moral decision making."],[1509.65,"It includes that."],[1510.39,"Because he talks about bearing fruit."],[1512.31,"He talks about being without offense."],[1514.17,"So that's talking about your own life and your moral reasoning."],[1516.97,"That's also talking about seeing what is good and what is excellent in others."],[1522.99,"That's part of discernment too."],[1526.11,"Looking at someone and finding fault is easy."],[1530.67,"It's very easy, especially if you're looking at me."],[1533.77,"It's very easy to find things wrong, find mistakes, find things to criticize."],[1536.73,"ind things. F not to like, we can all do it"],[1541.62,"It can often be more difficult to find the things that are good, that are excellent in people."],[1551.56,"That are always there that they might not even be aware of."],[1558.38,"And so part of how we build our love for each other is finding those things in people."],[1565.9,"And loving those things about them."],[1568.54,"And helping them to bring out those things in themselves to bear fruit."],[1576.54,"Because most of the time, when we go to people and we find fault with them. them and we condemn, even if it's legitimate"],[1584.08,"Even if there's legitimate sin in their life."],[1587.28,"Even if they're legitimately on the wrong path, and we come to them and we point it out and we condemn them for it, they know."],[1596.85,"I've never met a person who's an alcoholic or a drug addict who thinks it's great to be an alcoholic or a drug addict."],[1607.17,"I've never met someone who's living a truly sexually immoral lifestyle who doesn't know there's something wrong."],[1616.94,"They know. They don't, people don't need us to point that out to them."],[1622.26,"Because, like I said, it's easy to see."],[1625.66,"And if you're living in it and you're miserable, you know full well."],[1630.8,"But there are a lot of people out there who can't see anything good in themselves."],[1636.88,"Who can't see anything excellent in themselves or their life."],[1641.28,"And the only way to get them out of that misery and that place where they are is for them to find those good and excellent things and follow those."],[1652.45,"It doesn't matter telling somebody they're on the wrong road if you don't show them where the right road is and how to get there."],[1660.81,"Yeah, that reminds me of something we were taught, you know, as leaders in Kyopha the surprise and canvas misery."],[1669.13,"I forget what they called it."],[1670.49,"It was um uh affirmation sandwich or something."],[1675.45,"Basically you would affirm them, like you would tell them some of the things that they're doing good that you can see them doing."],[1682.01,"Then you would bring up, you know, what's going wrong, you would bring up what the problem is, but then you would also say, you know, here's how you can fix this, or you can, you know, I know you're capable of doing this, or you know."],[1695.47,"Yeah, and you you you don't."],[1698.01,"Again, this is discernment."],[1699.33,"You don't affirm the wrong things."],[1701.86,"That's what our culture says love is."],[1704.42,"You affirm everything no matter what."],[1707.3,"No matter how self-destructive, no matter how wrong."],[1710.1,"If you, because if you don't, you're a hater"],[1710.96,"You just affirm it, and that means you're loving."],[1718.17,"But you don't affirm what's wrong, but you do affirm what is good."],[1724.51,"You do affirm what is good."],[1727.15,"That's what St."],[1728.03,"Paul is aiming at."],[1729.65,"What's important is it's more important for us to see what is good and pursue it than to see what is bad and flee from it."],[1738.45,"Because if we're pursuing what is good, we'll we will be fleeing from what is evil."],[1744.61,"We saw this in Galatians, remember, with the fruit of the Spirit."],[1747.34,"He lists the fruit of the Spirit."],[1748.54,"He talks about fruit here again, the fruit of righteousness."],[1751.0,"The fruit of the spirit, love, joy, peace. patience, kindness, goodness, faithfulness, gentleness, self-control"],[1755.92,"Against such thing, there is no law."],[1760.58,"Because he's trying to say, stop worrying about did I do a bad thing?"],[1765.78,"Be so busy pursuing and doing good things that you don't even have time to think about it."],[1772.1,"Well, this was basic in teaching too, dealing with kids who were in trouble."],[1777.7,"They didn't need to be told that they done something wrong."],[1782.1,"Yeah, they knew they messed up."],[1783.54,"Yeah. I mean, some teachers would would do that with lecture, though, but that they don't need that."],[1790.58,"They feel awful people."],[1793.3,"And at that time, you've got to point out to them that they are not awful."],[1796.34,"that they are And that there are all these good things going for."],[1796.9,"They don't believe it"],[1803.5,"That they can do better."],[1805.18,"And they are better than that."],[1806.94,"That's the problem."],[1808.06,"Yeah. The problem with what you did is that you're better than that."],[1811.26,"That's what St."],[1811.9,"Paul's. That's why he starts out by calling them holy ones."],[1818.3,"If you're not acting like a holy one, that doesn't make that not true in Christ."],[1824.06,"It means that you're better than what you're doing right now."],[1829.58,"And so that's why and how you can change."],[1837.02,"Yeah, so that's that's what St."],[1838.94,"Paul is is making clear."],[1840.38,"And again, these themes are gonna play out. right in the in the rest"],[1844.5,"But love is based on knowledge and discernment."],[1850.02,"Those aren't opposed to being loving."],[1858.34,"Verse 12. But I want you to know, brethren, that the things which happened to me have actually turned out for the furtherance of the gospel."],[1867.78,"So that it has become evident to the whole palace guard and to all the rest that my chains are in Christ."],[1874.82,"And most of the brethren in the Lord, having become confident by my chains, are much more bold to speak the word without fear."],[1884.5,"So they know he's been imprisoned."],[1886.74,"And so he's saying this has actually worked out for good"],[1892.74,"I'm sure you're upset that I'm in prison and I'm suffering."],[1896.98,"You know. It's like, but this has actually worked out for good."],[1900.02,"Because it's even this has given St."],[1902.26,"Paul opportunities. To share the gospel with his guards."],[1907.12,"And uh with everyone that that um because he's already in jail."],[1915.94,"I mean, what are they gonna do?"],[1917.06,"Throw him in jail?"],[1919.7,"That's what he means by uh now I just speak freely."],[1922.82,"I mean, hey, I'm already in jail."],[1928.55,"Verse 15 Some indeed preach Christ even from envy and strife, and some also from good will."],[1936.39,"The former preach Christ from selfish ambition, not sincerely, supposing to add affliction to my chains, but the latter out of love, knowing that I am appointed for the defense of the gospel."],[1949.83,"What then, only that in every way, whether in pretense or in truth, Christ is preached, and in this I rejoice, yes, and will rejoice."],[1959.14,"So he says, you know, yeah, while I'm here in prison, there are a bunch of people who have heard the gospel from me who are going out and spreading it."],[1968.99,"And he says, Yeah, you know, some of them are ambitious."],[1972.35,"Some of them think, oh, St."],[1973.63,"Paul's locked up."],[1974.59,"I can take this position of prestige or something."],[1977.39,"I can, you know, while he's out of the way."],[1980.19,"I can, you know, make a name for myself and become important."],[1983.73,"So they're they're they're preaching the gospel, but they're doing it for the wrong reason."],[1987.81,"This never happens."],[1989.09,"Never happens today."],[1990.21,"There's nobody out there at all in Christian ministry who's doing it because they're out to get rich."],[1997.03,"I can't think of a single person."],[1999.19,"Um there's there's that happening, and he says, you know, there's other people out there who are doing it in goodwill."],[2006.63,"They're like, hey, St."],[2007.83,"Paul's locked up, I've got to carry on with his ministry, I've gotta, I've gotta preach the gospel."],[2014.79,"And St. Paul says, Hey, you know what?"],[2016.83,"Whether they're doing it because they hate me or out of rivalry or something, or whether they're doing it for the right reasons, at least the gospel's getting preached."],[2025.49,"People are hearing the gospel of Jesus Christ."],[2027.73,"They're coming to know Christ."],[2029.51,"Either in spite of the person preaching or because of it."],[2032.63,"And he says, and that's great."],[2035.2,"I rejoice. At least that's at least that's happening."],[2039.54,"And and this principle is a very good and uh and heartening one, uh, for people who are clergy."],[2049.48,"Because it means even if my motives aren't right, which they never quite are."],[2054.0,"Uh clearly I'm not in this to get rich, but uh um but uh uh you know even if my motives aren't right, which they never quite are, uh even if I mess up, even if I make mistakes."],[2070.21,"That doesn't restrict what God's able to do."],[2074.18,"Someone can go out and preach for all the wrong reasons, and God will still bring people to salvation for it."],[2082.34,"And through it."],[2083.59,"Um the people who Judas baptized didn't have to get baptized again."],[2089.32,"Um, I still wouldn't want to be one of those people."],[2092.6,"That would be messed up."],[2093.8,"But yeah, that would be messed up."],[2095.56,"But uh still they didn't have to be baptized again."],[2100.46,"Um, and so yeah, the the the goal is that people would come to know Christ."],[2107.74,"And you know, yeah, all of us who try to do that are sinful."],[2115.42,"But that doesn't stop, that doesn't stop anything uh from happening."],[2119.9,"That doesn't impose some barrier on God where he can't get it done."],[2127.9,"Verse 19 for I know that this will turn out for my deliverance through your prayer and the supply of the Spirit of Jesus Christ, according to my earnest expectation and hope that in nothing I shall be ashamed, but with all boldness, as always so now, also Christ will be magnified in my body, whether by life or by death."],[2144.94,"Okay So the reason I read so much is that I wanted to make the point that in verse 19 reads for I know that this will turn out for my deliverance."],[2161.22,"Deliverance there is not talking about getting let out of jail."],[2167.23,"He's not saying I know that I'm going to end up getting out of this jail and so I'm happy right because what does he say at the end of that sentence?"],[2176.35,"Christ will be magnified in my body whether by life or by death."],[2180.11,"So St. Paul's leaving open the impossibility he might die in prison."],[2184.53,"Or might be executed, which is what eventually happened in Rome."],[2188.29,"So that's not the deliverance he's talking about"],[2189.21,"He's leaving that possibility open."],[2192.57,"The deliverance he's talking about is his salvation."],[2196.65,"Which again, St."],[2197.85,"Paul does not think that in St."],[2199.69,"Paul's case was already accomplished."],[2205.37,"He doesn't think the work that was begun in him, like the work that was begun in the community in Philippi, was finished yet."],[2213.45,"It would be finished on the day of Jesus Christ."],[2217.05,"In his case also."],[2219.81,"So he says, this, me being in prison, everything that's happening, he says, I know is going to work toward my salvation."],[2229.65,"It's going to work toward my salvation."],[2233.17,"Whether I live, and I get let out of prison, whether I die in prison, then he says there'll be nothing in which he's ashamed because of how he's going to live however much life he has left in whatever circumstance it is in working out in working out his salvation."],[2255.11,"And what the principle St."],[2256.87,"Paul's talking about here, we've seen it before, uh, in the New Testament, is the idea that we can't control what happens to us in our life."],[2266.31,"We like to think so."],[2269.03,"We all very much like to think we're in control of everything."],[2272.31,"We're all control freaks to one degree or another."],[2275.27,"And we get very angry and very afraid and very upset when it becomes apparent that we're not."],[2278.55,"It's not that all of a sudden we're not"],[2285.95,"We never were."],[2287.07,"It's just all of a sudden we're forced to admit and to realize that we're not."],[2291.47,"And then we get afraid and angry and anxious."],[2295.05,"And all of these, all of these things happen."],[2297.85,"A lot of times that's with other people."],[2300.65,"We're able to live with the delusion that we're in control of ourselves."],[2304.01,"But as soon as you care about another person and you can't control what happens to them, and can't even control what decisions they make, all of a sudden there's a lot of anxiety and a lot of stress."],[2313.59,"Because we can't control it."],[2315.71,"Um, but we can't."],[2317.99,"The truth is we never can."],[2320.48,"But we can control how we receive things."],[2324.62,"I'm sure if you'd asked St."],[2325.82,"Paul, hey, would you like to get locked up in prison?"],[2329.34,"It's what would have been, well, no, I prefer not."],[2331.66,"I prefer to continue traveling and proclaiming the gospel."],[2337.15,"So that wouldn't have been his choice, but that's where he is."],[2341.31,"But how does St."],[2342.35,"Paul receive that?"],[2344.59,"Does he receive that with anger?"],[2348.03,"I gotta get out of this prison."],[2349.63,"Everybody pray that I get let out of prison."],[2351.55,"I gotta find a way out of this prison."],[2353.47,"God, why did you have me get locked up in prison?"],[2355.79,"I'm trying to preach the gospel and do what you want me to do, and now I'm in this prison."],[2360.8,"He doesn't get angry."],[2361.92,"He could be a sad sack."],[2364.17,"Why doesn't God care about me?"],[2365.69,"I'm in this prison."],[2368.02,"Could have whined and moaned."],[2369.47,"That's not how St."],[2370.35,"Paul receives it."],[2371.79,"That's not how he received it, remember, when he got locked up in Philippi."],[2375.47,"And he was singing hymns in the middle of the night while literally laying chained to the floor in his own waist with rats climbing around on him."],[2385.55,"He was singing hymns."],[2387.49,"But so that's not how he receives it."],[2389.25,"He says, I know that this is gonna work to my salvation."],[2393.65,"And he points to things he sees."],[2395.83,"Like, hey, I'm getting to preach to these guards."],[2397.99,"I'm getting to preach to these people who I wouldn't get to preach to otherwise."],[2402.95,"And you know what?"],[2403.83,"Since I'm locked up, these other people have been going out and preaching the gospel."],[2407.23,"And yeah, some of them are doing it for the wrong reason, but the gospel's getting preached now by a dozen people instead of just me."],[2416.83,"And I know that whatever's going on here, this is going to work to my salvation."],[2421.65,"This is going to help me repent of the things I need to repent of."],[2425.89,"This is giving me time to pray."],[2429.33,"He finds a way to receive it."],[2432.05,"And so this bad thing of being locked up in a prison becomes for him a good thing."],[2439.49,"Becomes for him a good thing because of how he receives it."],[2443.49,"He could receive it differently, and it could lead him to despair."],[2446.69,"It could lead him away from Christ."],[2449.73,"Or he could receive it the way he's receiving it."],[2451.41,"It can draw him closer to Christ, continue to change and transform him and work towards his salvation"],[2458.89,"And the same's true for us every day."],[2464.65,"Every day. Even little dumb stuff."],[2469.77,"Oh man, I'm gonna be late."],[2471.21,"Oh man, that guy cut me off in traffic."],[2475.69,"Oh, I didn't get that thing I wanted."],[2479.77,"Oh, I don't have enough money to get that thing I wanted."],[2482.01,"Or this, that, the other."],[2483.91,"We could choose to get angry, mope."],[2488.51,"Any of those things."],[2490.45,"But there is another option."],[2493.03,"There is another option that through prayer we come to see that whatever it is, whether it's a hardship, whether it's an imagined hardship, because it's really not that bad."],[2501.57,"Or whether it's a legit, I mean, being locked up in prison is a pretty legitimate hardship."],[2506.13,"Whether it's a legitimately horrible thing that happens to us, sometimes horrible things happen to us."],[2512.98,"But even in those horrible things, for example, uh, a horrible thing happens to us, well, that horrible thing happens to other people too."],[2525.86,"And maybe now that that horrible thing has happened to us, we can help minister to and help those people, those other people who it happens to."],[2534.18,"To support them and build them back up."],[2538.38,"Maybe if we fall into sin, horrible thing, not a good thing."],[2543.42,"Don't do it if you can help it."],[2545.98,"But if we fall into sin, and we have to struggle with it, and we have to fight our way out of it."],[2552.54,"Or even if we can't fight our way out of it, and we just have to struggle and struggle and struggle with it, there are other people out there struggling with the same thing."],[2561.02,"Who need our help and our support and our comfort, and to know that there are other people struggling with it too."],[2569.58,"So whatever it is, there are ways that we can turn it into the fruit of righteousness that St."],[2575.67,"Paul is talking about."],[2578.15,"There are ways that we can see it and receive it where we can eventually, maybe not in the moment that a horrible thing is happening to us, but eventually we can even thank God for it."],[2589.55,"Because of what it's enabled us to do in the future and who it's enabled us to become in the future."],[2596.83,"How it's drawn us closer to Him."],[2599.47,"So that's what St."],[2600.51,"Paul is sort of exemplifying for us here."],[2605.15,"Paul, again, it's not just this imprisonment."],[2607.31,"Remember, he gave that list."],[2609.25,"All the times he was stoned, shipwrecked, beaten, whipped and flawed."],[2614.79,"All of these things."],[2617.29,"But how he received them."],[2620.94,"So that even though the people who are doing all of those things to him meant it for evil."],[2625.66,"It became something good in his life"],[2632.22,"Not a good experience, but something that worked for his good down the road."],[2639.9,"And he had control of that."],[2641.82,"The people trying to hurt him, the people trying to kill him didn't have control over that."],[2650.62,"So who wins?"],[2652.14,"St. Paul wins."],[2654.86,"Not them. Verse 21."],[2662.3,"So he said that Christ will be magnified in his body, whether by life or by death."],[2666.3,"Verse 21, for to me to live is Christ and to die is gain."],[2670.14,"And he's going to expand on what he means by that"],[2676.74,"If I live on in the flesh, this will mean fruit from my labor."],[2686.16,"So to live is Christ, means I'll keep preaching the gospel, I'll keep working, and this will bear fruit."],[2692.82,"So bear fruit in his own life, in the communities that he touches, that's a good thing"],[2703.11,"Well, what if he dies?"],[2704.39,"What if he dies in prison?"],[2708.07,"Verse 23, for I am hard pressed between the two, having a desire to depart and be with Christ, which is far better."],[2715.11,"So if I die in prison, then I'll go and I'll be with Christ."],[2719.11,"That's a good thing, too."],[2722.44,"So Saint Paul has understood this idea of how we receive things so deeply."],[2729.52,"That St. Paul is invulnerable."],[2734.64,"No one can harm Saint Paul."],[2737.78,"No one can harm the righteous man."],[2740.42,"It's a saying of the fathers."],[2743.06,"Why? Well, what can they do?"],[2745.38,"If they kill him, he's martyred, he goes to be with Christ."],[2751.38,"If he lives on, even if he lives on in prison, he can preach the gospel."],[2755.74,"This is going to bear fruit in his life, it's going to bear fruit in other people's life."],[2760.46,"There's nothing anyone can do to him now."],[2765.5,"That will harm him."],[2767.6,"That will do him real harm."],[2769.6,"Nothing. That's what St."],[2771.68,"Paul's expressing. Yeah."],[2774.88,"And that's why he can rejoice."],[2778.1,"No matter what."],[2779.3,"Because they can't hurt him."],[2781.38,"He doesn't have anything he has to be afraid of."],[2785.4,"Because it will all it will all come to good."],[2792.68,"Verse 24: Nevertheless, to remain in the flesh is more needful for you."],[2797.81,"So he's saying it'd be better. for you for me to remain and be able to continue to know you and minister to you"],[2803.75,"Even though he might prefer to be with be with the Lord."],[2809.51,"Being confident of this, I know that I shall remain and continue with you all for your progress and joy of faith."],[2819.99,"Now, remember, faith should almost always be faithfulness."],[2825.72,"And faithfulness makes progress."],[2829.26,"How can you have progress of believing something to be true?"],[2833.66,"Doesn't work. Progress of your faithfulness as you continue this journey, this work that was begun in you, that's coming to conclusion."],[2841.57,"He's saying, I'm gonna I'm gonna continue for now at least, to be with you on this journey."],[2846.39,"That's what he's saying."],[2847.67,"And this is a journey of faithfulness and of joy."],[2851.77,"And of progress."],[2856.25,"That your rejoicing for me may be more abundant in Jesus Christ by my coming to you again."],[2861.87,"He's saying so, Lord willing, I'll get to come see you again, and then we'll rejoice even more."],[2867.93,"Because we'll be we'll be together again."],[2872.65,"Verse 27, only let your conduct be worthy of the gospel of Christ, so that whether I come and see you or am absent, I may hear of your affairs, that you stand fast in one spirit with one mind, striving together for the faithfulness, for faithfulness to the gospel."],[2894.13,"So he says, you need to continue down this road."],[2901.07,"You need to continue to progress."],[2902.67,"Whether I'm able to come be with you or whether I'm not."],[2905.71,"Whether I'm there, whether I'm not."],[2907.47,"If I'm not there, then I want to hear."],[2909.65,"If I'm there, I want to see it."],[2911.01,"If I'm not there, I want to hear about it."],[2913.65,"That you're together, you're of one mind, one heart, one spirit."],[2919.59,"The Holy Spirit, progressing together in Jesus Christ, and living in a way that is faithful."],[2927.19,"Verse 28, and not in any way terrified by your adversaries, which is to them a proof of perdition, but to you of salvation, and that from God."],[2940.83,"I'll continue to the end of the chapter."],[2942.51,"For to you it has been granted on behalf of Christ, not only to believe in him, but also to suffer for his sake, having the same conflict which you saw in me, and now here is in me."],[2953.71,"So he's just talked about his own situation in prison and how he receives things."],[2957.63,"Now he's telling them the same thing."],[2962.57,"He's saying, You are going to, in your life in Christ, as you continue on this journey, you're going to suffer things."],[2970.9,"It's like St."],[2971.62,"Paul is. It's not going to be, you know, everything coming up roses now that you've become a Christian"],[2978.72,"You're going to suffer too."],[2981.36,"And there are going to be adversaries."],[2985.25,"People who oppose it, people who are inflicting this suffering."],[2988.79,"And he says, to them."],[2991.13,"To them, it's going to be perition, condemnation."],[2995.06,"Evil. They're going to mean evil to you."],[2998.18,"But to you, salvation."],[3000.74,"And that from God, not from them."],[3003.08,"From God. So this is the same dynamic can be true."],[3009.44,"Based on how you suffer and how you receive it."],[3012.64,"Just as with Him"],[3017.02,"Because they're going to come to hurt you."],[3020.94,"They're going to come to harm you."],[3023.02,"To condemn you."],[3024.14,"To try to destroy you."],[3026.35,"Let's say Paul says, this could be for your salvation, and therefore you don't have to be terrified of them."],[3033.39,"You don't have to be afraid of them."],[3036.11,"That's the mark."],[3038.53,"That's the mark that you've understood this."],[3040.93,"And of how you're receiving them, that you don't have to be scared anymore."],[3046.85,"Of anybody, enemies visible or invisible, as our prayers say."],[3050.61,"You don't have to be scared of any of them, because there's nothing they can do to you."],[3055.33,"Because're of how you prepared to receive the sufferings that come into our lives necessarily."],[3075.67,"But this the joy and the fellowship and the love that he's so focused on is not based on an absence of suffering or of hardship or any of that."],[3086.87,"It's something that's continuous regardless."],[3089.99,"It comes from God, and that joy and peace is continuous regardless of whether things are good or bad."],[3097.99,"Regardless of whether he's stuck in a prison cell or whether he can come visit them and be with them and rejoice together."],[3104.69,"Regardless of sufferings, regardless of hardships."],[3107.51,"It's a continuous thing, it's a reality in Christ."],[3113.59,"So, end of chapter one's a good place to end."],[3118.47,"Chapter two is one of the most important chapters in the New Testament."],[3123.59,"I'll tease it for next week."],[3127.03,"So we'll come back next time we're talking about chapter two."],[3130.63,"Thank you, everybody. One"]],"o":[{"t":0.0,"lv":2,"h":"Philippians 1:1 - Slaves of Christ to the holy ones","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Reads <b>1:1</b>: \"Paul and Timothy, bond servants of Jesus Christ, to all the saints in Christ Jesus who are in Philippi, with the bishops and deacons\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Coming from <b>Paul and Timothy</b> - \"he has a co-author\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Bond servants</b> = \"a fancy way of saying <b>slaves</b>\" - slaves of Christ</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Saints</b> = <b>holy ones</b></li><li class=\"d1\">OT: language applied to the <b>angels</b>, the angelic beings</li><li class=\"d0\">Pattern in every epistle so far: opens by reminding them <b>who they are in Christ</b> -> who they're <b>called to be</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>sons of God</b>, <b>holy ones</b> - both OT angel language</li><li class=\"d1\">\"this is who you are in Christ, this is what you're called to be\"</li><li class=\"d1\">same in <b>1-2 Corinthians</b>, though not how they were acting - part of their calling</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul does the same here</li></ul>"},{"t":102.51,"lv":2,"h":"Bishops and deacons: the leadership","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">The difference: he addresses the <b>leadership</b> - <b>bishops and deacons</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><i>episcopos</i> = usually \"bishop\"; literally \"<b>overseer</b>\" in a very literalistic translation</li><li class=\"d1\"><i>scopos</i> = <b>scope</b> - \"not the mouthwash\" - telescope, to <b>see</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><i>epi</i> = <b>over or upon</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Greek OT uses it to translate a Hebrew word from a verb of seeing, \"to <b>watch over</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">used of <b>shepherds and herdsmen</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Not \"overseer\" like a <b>slave overseer or boss</b></li><li class=\"d1\">more like a <b>shepherd</b> -> why Latin gives <i>pastor</i></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>master/lord</b> idea would be another word: <i>despotes</i>, <i>kyrios</i></li></ul>"},{"t":207.9,"lv":3,"h":"Shepherds and servants","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Rich <b>OT</b> background: leaders of Israel = <b>shepherds of Israel</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not the owners - \"God's sheep,\" but he sent shepherds to <b>watch over</b> them</li><li class=\"d0\">Same idea in \"bishop\": \"you're the shepherds, the herdsmen over <b>Christ's flock</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Deacon</b> = <i>diakonia</i>, <b>service</b>; <i>diakonos</i> = servant</li><li class=\"d0\">Two roles:</li><li class=\"d1\">those <b>serving</b> in the community</li><li class=\"d1\">those assigned to <b>oversee, guide, protect</b></li></ul>"},{"t":266.68,"lv":3,"h":"Why no presbyters?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Interesting: Paul <b>doesn't include the presbyters</b> - \"we can never say for sure why\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Presbyters existed; role at this point: every community has an <i>episcopos</i> as <b>head presbyter</b>, with a <b>plurality of presbyters</b> around</li><li class=\"d1\">how differentiated the bishop was: <b>different in different communities</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Rome</b> until ~3rd c.: basically head presbyter - \"sorry, Roman Catholics, I'll pick on them this time\"; no strong episcopate in 1st-2nd c.</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Alexandria</b>, into 4th c.: bishop had <b>less power</b> than presbyters meeting in council</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Antioch</b>, end of 1st c., <b>St. Ignatius</b>: episcopal office clearly <b>unique</b>, unique authority</li><li class=\"d1\">presbyters exercise <b>his authority in his absence</b> - as in the Orthodox Church today</li><li class=\"d0\">Protestant seminary prof: by Ignatius \"all the features of the <b>magisterial bishop</b> - how did things go so wrong so fast?\"</li><li class=\"d1\">his reply even then: \"there's another way of interpreting that\"</li><li class=\"d0\">So in <b>Philippi</b> maybe no strong separate presbyter role</li><li class=\"d0\">Or Paul uses \"bishops\" as a <b>general term for leadership</b> - includes presbyters, bishop a head presbyter</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Hard to know for sure\"</li></ul>"},{"t":484.79,"lv":3,"h":"Apostles still around","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Part of the variety: in 1st c. the <b>apostles were still around</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Acts: Paul at <b>Ephesus</b> on way to Jerusalem meets the <b>presbyters</b></li><li class=\"d1\">why no bishop at Ephesus? <b>multiple apostles</b> coming and going: <b>Paul</b>, <b>St. John</b> around Ephesus, <b>Mary Magdalene</b>, the <b>Theotokos</b> for a while</li><li class=\"d0\">With <b>apostolic leadership</b>, always presbyters - not necessarily a bishop</li><li class=\"d0\">Histories call bishops \"<b>successors to the apostles</b>\": episcopacy <b>consolidates and takes off</b> once the apostles are gone</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>1-2 Timothy</b> ahead: Paul sends <b>Timothy to Crete</b> - appoint <b>presbyters and deacons</b> in every community</li><li class=\"d1\">Timothy: really an <b>apostle</b>, one of the <b>70</b> - also fulfilling the <b>episcopal office</b></li><li class=\"d1\">clearly not a presbyter - he <b>appoints</b> presbyters; \"clearly something else\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>pivotal figure</b>: apostle in one sense, bishop in another -> successors just bishops</li></ul>"},{"t":600.61,"lv":3,"h":"Malleable offices; leadership addressed","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Conclusion: don't know exactly; likely the <b>precise offices were malleable</b> in any particular place</li><li class=\"d0\">Also: only Pauline epistle that <b>directly mentions leadership in the opening</b></li><li class=\"d1\">leaders mentioned later in other places</li><li class=\"d0\">Tied to the tone: more positive, <b>focused on teaching</b></li><li class=\"d1\">makes sense to direct it to the <b>leaders who will teach the people</b></li></ul>"},{"t":658.07,"lv":2,"h":"Philippians 1:2-5 - Thanks with joy","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>1:2</b>: \"grace to you and peace from God our Father and the Lord Jesus Christ\" - standard opening greeting</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>1:3-5</b>: \"I thank my God upon every remembrance of you ... with joy, for your <b>fellowship in the gospel</b> from the first day until now\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Different tone, not:</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Galatians</b>: \"how readily you've received another gospel\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>1 Corinthians 1</b>: unrepentant sexual liberality - \"what's up with that?\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Romans 1</b>: wrath of God revealed on immorality</li><li class=\"d0\">Here: <b>giving thanks with joy</b></li><li class=\"d1\">every remembrance, every prayer - mentions Philippians, joyfully requests good for them</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Fellowship</b> = <i>koinonia</i>, <b>communion</b> in the gospel</li><li class=\"d1\">from the <b>first day</b> he went there until now</li><li class=\"d1\">significant: some communities he couldn't say this to</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Rome</b>: hadn't met them yet</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Corinth</b>: knew them well, but communion <b>broken</b> by what was going on</li><li class=\"d1\">Philippi: <b>unbroken communion</b> from the very beginning - he rejoices</li></ul>"},{"t":788.33,"lv":2,"h":"Philippians 1:6 - The day of Jesus Christ","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>1:6</b>: \"he who has begun a good work in you will complete it until the <b>day of Jesus Christ</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Day of Jesus Christ\" = the <b>last day</b>, Christ's return, day of the Lord - \"we know instinctively\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Why significant: in OT <b>Hebrew</b> it's the \"<b>day of Yahweh</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">the last day, the <b>day of judgment</b> = day of Yahweh, the God of Israel</li><li class=\"d1\">Greek doesn't transliterate <b>Yahweh</b> - put <b>Lord, <i>Kyrios</i></b> as replacement</li><li class=\"d1\">NT \"day of the Lord\" therefore <b>ambiguous</b>: the Lord Jesus, or Yahweh?</li><li class=\"d0\">Here Paul calls the <b>day of Yahweh</b> the day of <b>Jesus Christ</b></li><li class=\"d1\">using them <b>interchangeably</b> -> tells us who Paul thinks Jesus is</li><li class=\"d1\">for Orthodox, Jesus is God - \"not a big deal\"; a <b>skeptical modern theologian</b> would reject that Paul thought it</li><li class=\"d1\">one more piece of evidence, of a bunch in the epistles: not just Son of God or \"connected to\" Yahweh - <b>identifies Jesus with Yahweh</b></li></ul>"},{"t":954.62,"lv":3,"h":"Salvation begun, not finished","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Audio picks up mid-thought: contrast with \"you got saved, I got saved on the road to Damascus, you made a <b>decision</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Is that what he says? No: fellowship in the gospel = <b>God has begun a good work</b> in you</li><li class=\"d1\">God began your <b>salvation</b> in you</li><li class=\"d1\">brings it to completion when?</li><li class=\"d1\">not \"once you get <b>baptized</b>\" - no</li><li class=\"d1\">not \"once you do enough <b>good works</b>\" - no</li><li class=\"d1\">not \"once you're in the <b>state of grace</b>\" - no</li><li class=\"d1\">on the <b>day of the Lord</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"Until then, we're working on it\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul <b>drops themes</b> into the beginning of letters - this one expanded in <b>chapter 3</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Salvation not a thing that happened <b>once</b></li><li class=\"d1\">begins at a point in time, carries through life and <b>into the next life at the resurrection</b> - brought to completion</li></ul>"},{"t":1055.4,"lv":2,"h":"Philippians 1:7-8 - Partakers of grace","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>1:7</b>: \"right for me to think this of you all, because I have you <b>in my heart</b> ... both in my <b>chains</b> and in the defense and confirmation of the gospel, you all are <b>partakers with me of grace</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul: you're in my heart whether I'm <b>in chains</b> or out <b>preaching, traveling</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Grace</b> = \"God <b>acting</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Christ began the work in you, working in my life - in a prison or preaching</li><li class=\"d1\">\"we have this in common\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Communion in the gospel = God working in <b>all of us and each of us</b> to bring <b>salvation to completion</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"all on this road together, headed in the same direction\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Gives Paul <b>unity</b> with them and <b>rejoicing</b> regardless of circumstances</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>1:8</b>: \"how greatly I long for you all with the <b>affection of Jesus Christ</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">wishes to be with them in person - not just because he's in prison; these are people he <b>knows</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1163.82,"lv":2,"h":"Philippians 1:9 - Love in knowledge and discernment","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>1:9</b>: \"this I pray, that your <b>love</b> may abound still more and more in <b>knowledge</b> and all <b>discernment</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Requests made with joy - now <b>what he asks</b>: love abounding, for each other and for God</li><li class=\"d0\">Love takes the form of <b>knowledge and discernment</b></li><li class=\"d1\">matters given the \"<b>lazy way</b> we use the term love in the modern world\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Modern idea: loving = <b>ignorant and undiscerning</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"more love in our community\" = welcome anybody, no matter who, what they do, how they live</li><li class=\"d1\">to do that: <b>don't ask questions</b>, \"we just don't want to know\" - might make it harder to love you</li><li class=\"d1\">don't talk about anything upsetting, <b>never confront</b> -> \"now we're super loving\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Opposite</b> of what Paul means - \"that's why he qualifies it\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1291.3,"lv":3,"h":"Knowing God, knowing each other","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Love and knowledge: can't love God more without <b>coming to know him</b> more</li><li class=\"d1\">not knowledge \"of brain,\" <b>learning facts</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Example (used before): people know more facts about <b>his wife</b> than he does</li><li class=\"d1\">her <b>doctor</b>: blood levels, exact weight and height to the fraction of an inch</li><li class=\"d1\">people who've known her longer, know her childhood</li><li class=\"d1\">but \"I'd bet money no one <b>knows her better</b>\" - knowing a person vs. a bunch of <b>facts</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Not \"learn the attributes of God, God as <b>pure act</b>, <b>divine simplicity</b> and the <b>essence-energies</b> distinction\"</li><li class=\"d1\">not what we call <b>theology</b> - coming to know <b>God, Christ</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>St. Gregory Palamas</b>: doesn't require going to <b>school</b> even a day</li><li class=\"d0\">Also knowledge <b>toward each other</b></li><li class=\"d1\">love not based on <b>ignoring</b> things or deliberately not asking questions we may not like the answer to</li><li class=\"d1\">getting to know each other better and love each other</li></ul>"},{"t":1429.01,"lv":2,"h":"Philippians 1:10-11 - Discernment is knowing the good","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Discernment</b> = opposite of \"just accepting whatever\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>1:10-11</b>: \"approve the things that are <b>excellent</b>, that you may be <b>sincere and without offense</b> till the day of Christ, being filled with the <b>fruits of righteousness</b> ... to the glory and praise of God\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Positive tone: discernment isn't just <b>identifying what is bad</b></li><li class=\"d0\">False dilemma people pose:</li><li class=\"d1\">either loving and <b>accept whatever</b></li><li class=\"d1\">or <b>judgmental</b>, pointing out faults - \"shady\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Discernment = <b>knowing what is good</b>: excellent, pure, holy - identifying and <b>seeking after</b> it</li><li class=\"d1\">in your own life and <b>moral decision making</b> - bearing fruit, without offense</li><li class=\"d1\">also <b>seeing what is good and excellent in others</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Finding fault is <b>easy</b> - \"especially if you're looking at me\"</li><li class=\"d1\">finding what's <b>good and excellent</b> in people is harder - always there, they may not be aware</li><li class=\"d1\">part of building love: <b>find those things, love them, help them bear fruit</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1576.54,"lv":3,"h":"Pointing out faults doesn't help","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Going to people to find fault and condemn - even if <b>legitimate sin</b>, legitimately on the wrong path - <b>they know</b></li><li class=\"d1\">never met an <b>alcoholic or drug addict</b> who thinks it's great</li><li class=\"d1\">\"people don't need us to point that out\" - living in it, miserable, know full well</li><li class=\"d0\">Many can't see <b>anything good</b> in themselves or their life</li><li class=\"d1\">only way out of the misery: <b>find the good and excellent things and follow them</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"Doesn't matter telling somebody they're on the wrong road if you don't show them <b>where the right road is</b> and how to get there\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1660.81,"lv":3,"h":"Audience: the affirmation sandwich","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Audience comment: leadership training - \"<b>affirmation sandwich</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">affirm what they're doing <b>good</b></li><li class=\"d1\">bring up what's going wrong</li><li class=\"d1\">say \"here's how you can fix this,\" \"I know you're capable\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Fr Stephen: <b>this is discernment</b></li><li class=\"d1\">don't <b>affirm the wrong things</b> - what \"our culture says love is\": affirm everything, however <b>self-destructive</b> -> if you don't, \"you're a <b>hater</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">don't affirm what's wrong, <b>do</b> affirm what is good</li><li class=\"d0\">That's what Paul is aiming at</li><li class=\"d1\">more important to <b>see and pursue the good</b> than see the bad and flee - pursuing good means fleeing evil</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Galatians</b>: fruit of the Spirit - love, joy, peace, patience, kindness, goodness, faithfulness, gentleness, self-control - \"against such there is no law\"</li><li class=\"d1\">here the <b>fruit of righteousness</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Stop worrying \"did I do a bad thing\" - \"be so busy pursuing and doing good things that you don't even have time to think about it\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1772.1,"lv":3,"h":"Audience: kids in trouble; 'you're better than that'","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Audience (teacher): basic in teaching, <b>kids in trouble</b></li><li class=\"d1\">didn't need to be told they did wrong - they knew; \"feel awful\"</li><li class=\"d1\">some teachers <b>lecture</b> anyway</li><li class=\"d1\">need to be told they are <b>not awful</b>, there are good things they're going for, they can do <b>better</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"they don't believe it\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Fr Stephen: \"the problem with what you did is that <b>you're better than that</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">why Paul starts by calling them <b>holy ones</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not acting like a holy one doesn't make it not true <b>in Christ</b> - means you're better than what you're doing now</li><li class=\"d1\">\"that's why and how you can <b>change</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Themes will play out in the rest of the letter</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Love is based on knowledge and discernment</b> - not opposed to being loving</li></ul>"},{"t":1858.34,"lv":2,"h":"Philippians 1:12-14 - Chains advance the gospel","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>1:12-14</b>: \"the things which happened to me have actually turned out for the <b>furtherance of the gospel</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">evident to the whole <b>palace guard</b> and all the rest that his chains are <b>in Christ</b></li><li class=\"d1\">most of the brethren, <b>confident by my chains</b>, \"much more bold to speak the word without fear\"</li><li class=\"d0\">They know he's imprisoned - \"I'm sure you're upset that I'm in prison and suffering\"</li><li class=\"d1\">actually worked out for <b>good</b></li><li class=\"d1\">opportunity to share the gospel with his <b>guards</b> and everyone</li><li class=\"d0\">\"<b>Already in jail</b> - what are they gonna do? Throw him in jail?\"</li><li class=\"d1\">that's what he means by now speaking <b>freely</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1928.55,"lv":2,"h":"Philippians 1:15-18 - Preaching from envy or goodwill","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>1:15-18</b>: some preach Christ \"from <b>envy and strife</b>,\" some from <b>goodwill</b></li><li class=\"d1\">former: <b>selfish ambition</b>, \"supposing to add affliction to my chains\"</li><li class=\"d1\">latter: <b>love</b>, knowing he's appointed for the <b>defense of the gospel</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"whether in pretense or in truth, <b>Christ is preached</b>, and in this I rejoice\"</li><li class=\"d0\">People who heard the gospel from him are spreading it</li><li class=\"d0\">Some <b>ambitious</b>: \"Paul's locked up, I can take this position of prestige\" - make a name while he's out of the way</li><li class=\"d1\">\"this never happens today ... nobody in Christian ministry out to <b>get rich</b>\" (sarcasm)</li><li class=\"d0\">Others: \"Paul's locked up, I gotta <b>carry on his ministry</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul: whether <b>hate, rivalry</b>, or right reasons, <b>at least the gospel's preached</b></li><li class=\"d1\">people come to know Christ \"in spite of the person preaching or because of it\" -> \"I rejoice\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2039.54,"lv":3,"h":"Encouragement for clergy; Judas' baptisms","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Principle \"very good and <b>heartening</b>\" for <b>clergy</b></li><li class=\"d1\">even if my motives aren't right, \"which they never quite are\" - clearly not in it for the money</li><li class=\"d1\">even if I mess up, make mistakes: <b>doesn't restrict what God is able to do</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Someone can preach for <b>all the wrong reasons</b>, God still brings people to <b>salvation</b> through it</li><li class=\"d1\">people <b>Judas baptized</b> didn't have to be <b>baptized again</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"I still wouldn't want to be one of those people\" - \"that would be messed up\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Goal: people come to <b>know Christ</b></li><li class=\"d1\">all who try are <b>sinful</b>, but doesn't impose a <b>barrier</b> on God</li></ul>"},{"t":2127.9,"lv":2,"h":"Philippians 1:19-20 - 'Deliverance' means salvation","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>1:19-20</b>: \"this will turn out for my <b>deliverance</b> through your <b>prayer</b> and the supply of the <b>Spirit of Jesus Christ</b> ... in nothing I shall be ashamed, but with all <b>boldness</b> ... Christ will be <b>magnified in my body</b>, whether by <b>life or by death</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Deliverance</b> is not <b>getting let out of jail</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"Christ magnified ... whether by life or death\" - he leaves open <b>dying in prison</b>, or being <b>executed</b> - what eventually happened in Rome</li><li class=\"d0\">Deliverance = his <b>salvation</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Paul doesn't think his was already accomplished</li><li class=\"d1\">work begun in him, like in Philippi, <b>not finished</b> until the <b>day of Jesus Christ</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2233.17,"lv":3,"h":"Controlling how we receive things","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Whether he dies in prison or not - nothing to be <b>ashamed</b> of in how he lives, in whatever circumstance, <b>working out his salvation</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Principle: we <b>can't control what happens to us</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"we all very much like to think we're in control\" - <b>control freaks</b> to one degree or another</li><li class=\"d1\">angry, afraid, upset when it's apparent we're not - \"we never were,\" just forced to <b>admit</b> it</li><li class=\"d1\">often with <b>other people</b>: delusion of controlling ourselves until we care about someone and can't control what happens or even their <b>decisions</b> -> anxiety and stress</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>We can control how we receive things</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Paul wouldn't have chosen prison - preferred <b>traveling and proclaiming</b></li><li class=\"d1\">receives it not with <b>anger</b> (\"everybody pray I get let out,\" \"God, why did you have me get locked up\")</li><li class=\"d1\">not as a <b>sad sack</b>, whining</li><li class=\"d1\">same in <b>Philippi</b>: <b>singing hymns</b> at night, chained to the floor \"with rats climbing around\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2389.25,"lv":3,"h":"Prison works to his salvation","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Paul: \"I know this is going to work to my <b>salvation</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Points to what he sees:</li><li class=\"d1\">getting to preach to the <b>guards</b>, people he wouldn't otherwise</li><li class=\"d1\">locked up -> others preaching: \"by a <b>dozen people</b> instead of just me,\" even if wrong reasons</li><li class=\"d0\">Whatever's going on:</li><li class=\"d1\">helps him <b>repent</b> of what he needs to repent</li><li class=\"d1\">gives him <b>time to pray</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"He finds a way to receive it\" -> bad thing becomes a <b>good thing</b> because of <b>how he receives it</b></li><li class=\"d1\">could lead to <b>despair</b> and away from Christ, or draw him <b>closer to Christ</b>, continuing to <b>transform</b> him</li></ul>"},{"t":2458.89,"lv":3,"h":"Every day: receiving hardship","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Same true for us every day - \"even <b>little dumb stuff</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"I'm gonna be late,\" \"that guy cut me off,\" \"didn't get that thing I wanted,\" not enough money</li><li class=\"d1\">could choose <b>anger</b>, moping</li><li class=\"d1\">\"another option\": through <b>prayer</b> see it differently</li><li class=\"d0\">Imagined hardship (\"not really that bad\") or <b>legit</b> - \"being locked up in prison is a pretty legitimate hardship\"</li><li class=\"d1\">horrible things happen to others too -> now we can <b>minister to them</b>, support, build them back up</li><li class=\"d1\">if we <b>fall into sin</b> - \"don't do it if you can help it\" - and struggle, even can't fight out of it</li><li class=\"d1\">others struggle with the same, need <b>help, comfort</b>, to know they're not alone</li><li class=\"d0\">Ways to turn it into the <b>fruit of righteousness</b></li><li class=\"d1\">eventually, \"maybe not in the moment,\" we can even <b>thank God</b> for it</li><li class=\"d1\">for what it enables us to do and <b>become</b>; how it drew us closer to him</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul exemplifies: not just this imprisonment</li><li class=\"d1\">his <b>list</b>: stoned, shipwrecked, beaten, whipped - it's <b>how he received them</b></li><li class=\"d1\">though others meant it for <b>evil</b>, worked for his good - \"not a good experience\"</li><li class=\"d1\">he had control of that; they didn't - \"who wins? <b>St. Paul wins</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2654.86,"lv":2,"h":"Philippians 1:21-23 - To live is Christ, to die is gain","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>1:21</b>: \"for to me to <b>live is Christ</b> and to <b>die is gain</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Expands:</li><li class=\"d1\">\"if I live on in the flesh, this will mean <b>fruit from my labor</b>\" - keep preaching, working</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>fruit</b> in his own life, the communities he touches, the world - \"a good thing\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>1:23</b>: \"hard pressed between the two, having a desire to <b>depart and be with Christ</b>, which is far better\"</li><li class=\"d1\">if he dies in prison, he's with Christ - \"that's a good thing, too\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2722.44,"lv":3,"h":"The righteous man is invulnerable","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Paul understood how we receive things so deeply that he is <b>invulnerable</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"<b>no one can harm the righteous man</b>\" - saying of the fathers</li><li class=\"d1\">if they kill him: <b>martyred</b>, goes to be with Christ</li><li class=\"d1\">if he lives on, even in prison: <b>preaches the gospel</b>, bears fruit in his life and others'</li><li class=\"d1\">nothing anyone can do that will do him <b>real harm</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"He's <b>dead to them</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">why he can <b>rejoice no matter what</b> - nothing to be afraid of, \"it will all come to <b>good</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2792.68,"lv":2,"h":"Philippians 1:24-26 - Remaining for your progress","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>1:24</b>: \"to remain in the flesh is <b>more needful for you</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">better for them that he remain, continue to know and minister to them - though he might prefer to be with the Lord</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>1:25</b>: confident he'll \"remain and continue with you all for your <b>progress and joy of faith</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>faith</b> should \"almost always be <b>faithfulness</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">faithfulness <b>makes progress</b>; \"progress of believing something to be true\" doesn't work</li><li class=\"d1\">a <b>journey</b>, work begun in you coming to conclusion; he'll continue <b>for now at least</b> on this journey</li><li class=\"d1\">journey of faithfulness, <b>joy</b>, progress</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>1:26</b>: \"your rejoicing for me may be more abundant in Jesus Christ by my <b>coming to you again</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Lord willing, he'll see them again - rejoice more together</li></ul>"},{"t":2872.65,"lv":2,"h":"Philippians 1:27 - Conduct worthy of the gospel","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>1:27</b>: \"let your conduct be worthy of the gospel of Christ ... whether I come and see you or am absent, I may hear of your affairs, that you <b>stand fast in one spirit with one mind</b>, striving together for <b>faithfulness to the gospel</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Keep going down this road, keep <b>progressing</b></li><li class=\"d1\">whether he's there or not</li><li class=\"d1\">there: want to <b>see</b> it; not there: want to <b>hear</b> about it</li><li class=\"d0\">Together: <b>one mind, one heart, one spirit</b> - the <b>Holy Spirit</b></li><li class=\"d1\">progressing together in Jesus Christ, living <b>faithfully</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2927.19,"lv":2,"h":"Philippians 1:28-30 - Granted to suffer","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>1:28-30</b>: \"not in any way <b>terrified by your adversaries</b>, which is to them a proof of <b>perdition</b>, but to you of <b>salvation</b>, and that from God ... granted on behalf of Christ, not only to believe in him, but also to <b>suffer for his sake</b>, having the same conflict which you saw in me, and now here is in me\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Just talked about his own prison and how he receives it - now says <b>the same to them</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not \"everything coming up roses now that you've become a Christian\" - <b>you're going to suffer too</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>adversaries</b> oppose, inflict suffering</li><li class=\"d1\">to them: <b>perdition, condemnation, evil</b> - they mean evil to you</li><li class=\"d1\">to you: <b>salvation</b> - \"and that from <b>God</b>, not from that\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Same dynamic as Paul: <b>how you suffer and receive it</b></li><li class=\"d1\">they come to harm, condemn, destroy -> but could be for your salvation -> <b>don't have to be terrified</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"that's the <b>mark</b> that you've understood this\" - not scared of anybody, \"enemies <b>visible or invisible</b>, as our prayers say\"</li><li class=\"d1\">nothing they can do - because you're prepared to receive the sufferings that necessarily come</li></ul>"},{"t":3075.67,"lv":3,"h":"Joy continuous regardless; end of chapter 1","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">The <b>joy, fellowship, love</b> he's focused on is <b>not based on absence of suffering</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>continuous regardless</b> - from <b>God</b></li><li class=\"d1\">joy and peace whether things good or bad; <b>prison cell</b> or visiting them and rejoicing together</li><li class=\"d1\">\"a reality in <b>Christ</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">End of chapter 1 \"a good place to end\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Chapter 2</b> = \"one of the <b>most important chapters in the New Testament</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"I'll tease it for next week\"</li></ul>"}],"next":"Next time: <b>chapter 2</b> - \"one of the most important chapters in the New Testament\""});
